{
 "method_card": {
  "steps": [
   "For pressure from force: p = F ÷ A (pascals, Pa). Convert area to m² first",
   "For pressure at depth: p = hρg. Convert h to metres, ρ to kg/m³, use g = 10 N/kg",
   "To find force from pressure: F = p × A",
   "An object floats when upthrust = weight"
  ],
  "title": "Pressure in Fluids",
  "content": "<p>Two pressure equations — choose the right one for the context.</p><p><strong>p = F / A:</strong> Use when you have force and area (or need to find one of them). Units: Pa = N/m². Area must be in m².</p><p><strong>p = hρg:</strong> Use when you have depth in a liquid. h = depth in m, ρ = density in kg/m³ (water = 1000, seawater ≈ 1025), g = 10 N/kg.</p><p><strong>Common pitfalls:</strong></p><ul><li>Convert cm to m (divide by 100) and g/cm³ to kg/m³ (multiply by 1000) before substituting</li><li>p = hρg gives pressure due to the fluid column only, not total pressure (which also includes atmospheric pressure)</li></ul>"
 },
 "topic_links": {
  "prerequisites": []
 },
 "exam_context": {
  "marks": "3–5 per calculation",
  "paper": "Paper 2 (Physics)",
  "frequency": "Moderate — pressure calculations appear in higher-tier physics papers"
 },
